>From James Sainsbury
If you have gnu date installed its fairly simple

 	TOMORROW=3D`date +%d -d tomorrow`
 	if [ "$TOMORROW" -eq 1 ]; then
 		# today is the last day of this month

Failing that since you are in the US (where from our point of view it is =
always yesterday:) you can try setting the TZ variable to timezone ~ 24 =
hour ahead of yours.
eg
 	# if your timezone is GMT+7 then
 	TOMORROW=3D`TZ=3DGMT-17 date +%d`

You could work out the current offset from GMT for your locale and and =
24 hours in the script.




