>From Chris Wincentsen
Hi Blake,

I do this little "case" to check dates.  You could modify this kind of =
logic for your needs...

#!/bin/ksh

cur_day=3D`date "+%d"`
cur_mon=3D`date "+%b"`
cur_mon_day=3D`date "+%b %d"`
date_var=3D""

# * Process last day of month...
case "$cur_mon"
in
    "Jan") date_var=3D" Jan 31" ;;
    "Feb")
           date_var=3D" Feb 29"
           check_29th=3D`last | grep -s " Feb 29"`
           if [ -z "$check_29th" ]
           then
             date_var=3D" Feb 28"
           fi
           ;;
    "Mar") date_var=3D" Mar 31" ;;
    "Apr") date_var=3D" Apr 30" ;;
    "May") date_var=3D" May 31" ;;
    "Jun") date_var=3D" Jun 30" ;;
    "Jul") date_var=3D" Jul 31" ;;
    "Aug") date_var=3D" Aug 31" ;;
    "Sep") date_var=3D" Sep 30" ;;
    "Oct") date_var=3D" Oct 31" ;;
    "Nov") date_var=3D" Nov 30" ;;
    "Dec") date_var=3D" Dec 31" ;;
        *)
#          * Problem, set error value and exit...
           echo " Problem setting date variable."
           date_var=3D"error"
           exit 1
           ;;
esac

if [ "$date_var" =3D "$cur_mon_day" ]
then
# * Process last day of month...
# submit job here
fi

----------------------------------------

The Feb 29th processing probably needs work for what you want.  I =
actually look for the last day of the previous month, so I've changed my =
logic to be closer to what you might need.

Anyway, hope that helps!

Chris

